{"retrieved_on": "2026-11-13", "source_url": "https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=%28EXT_ID%3A10833167+OR+EXT_ID%3A10858586+OR+EXT_ID%3A11381101+OR+EXT_ID%3A11585785+OR+EXT_ID%3A11932229+OR+EXT_ID%3A14974593+OR+EXT_ID%3A15109499+OR+EXT_ID%3A15333167+OR+EXT_ID%3A15377225+OR+EXT_ID%3A15961328+OR+EXT_ID%3A16402624+OR+EXT_ID%3A16823476+OR+EXT_ID%3A16894993+OR+EXT_ID%3A16953201+OR+EXT_ID%3A17296637+OR+EXT_ID%3A18190724+OR+EXT_ID%3A18263565+OR+EXT_ID%3A18625687+OR+EXT_ID%3A18798884+OR+EXT_ID%3A18923511+OR+EXT_ID%3A19013264+OR+EXT_ID%3A19033363+OR+EXT_ID%3A19036144+OR+EXT_ID%3A19180106+OR+EXT_ID%3A19192942+OR+EXT_ID%3A19215827+OR+EXT_ID%3A19239892+OR+EXT_ID%3A19247697+OR+EXT_ID%3A19545393+OR+EXT_ID%3A19621070+OR+EXT_ID%3A19622552+OR+EXT_ID%3A19632885+OR+EXT_ID%3A19859947+OR+EXT_ID%3A19945040+OR+EXT_ID%3A20001796+OR+EXT_ID%3A20010215+OR+EXT_ID%3A20060481+OR+EXT_ID%3A20064248+OR+EXT_ID%3A20161266+OR+EXT_ID%3A20169112+OR+EXT_ID%3A20332509+OR+EXT_ID%3A20334632+OR+EXT_ID%3A20334633+OR+EXT_ID%3A20352064+OR+EXT_ID%3A20353579+OR+EXT_ID%3A20394896+OR+EXT_ID%3A20420659+OR+EXT_ID%3A20484781+OR+EXT_ID%3A20543992+OR+EXT_ID%3A20574967+OR+EXT_ID%3A20671804+OR+EXT_ID%3A20680289+OR+EXT_ID%3A20831489+OR+EXT_ID%3A21179089+OR+EXT_ID%3A21235751+OR+EXT_ID%3A21350618+OR+EXT_ID%3A21452728+OR+EXT_ID%3A21531085+OR+EXT_ID%3A21633166+OR+EXT_ID%3A21647869%29+AND+SRC%3AMED&format=json&resultType=core&pageSize=100", "hitCount": 1, "resultList": {"result": [{"id": "20831489", "source": "MED", "pmid": "20831489", "pmcid": "PMC4091820", "fullTextIdList": {"fullTextId": ["PMC4091820"]}, "doi": "10.3109/0284186x.2010.498437", "title": "Variability of textural features in FDG PET images due to different acquisition modes and reconstruction parameters.", "authorString": "Galavis PE, Hollensen C, Jallow N, Paliwal B, Jeraj R.", "authorList": {"author": [{"fullName": "Galavis PE", "lastName": "Galavis", "firstName": "Paulina E", "initials": "PE", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2154-2488"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Medical Physics, University of Wisconsin, Madison, WI, USA. galavis@wisc.edu"}]}}, {"fullName": "Hollensen C", "lastName": "Hollensen", "firstName": "Christian", "initials": "C"}, {"fullName": "Jallow N", "lastName": "Jallow", "firstName": "Ngoneh", "initials": "N"}, {"fullName": "Paliwal B", "lastName": "Paliwal", "firstName": "Bhudatt", "initials": "B"}, {"fullName": "Jeraj R", "lastName": "Jeraj", "firstName": "Robert", "initials": "R", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2192-2931"}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2154-2488"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2192-2931"}]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "7", "volume": "49", "journalIssueId": 1761338, "dateOfPublication": "2010 Oct", "monthOfPublication": 10, "yearOfPublication": 2010, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Acta Oncol", "title": "Acta oncologica (Stockholm, Sweden)", "essn": "1651-226X", "issn": "0284-186X", "nlmid": "8709065", "isoabbreviation": "Acta Oncol"}, "printPublicationDate": "2010-10-01"}, "pubYear": "2010", "pageInfo": "1012-1016", "abstractText": "<h4>Background</h4>Characterization of textural features (spatial distributions of image intensity levels) has been considered as a tool for automatic tumor segmentation. The purpose of this work is to study the variability of the textural features in PET images due to different acquisition modes and reconstruction parameters.<h4>Material and methods</h4>Twenty patients with solid tumors underwent PET/CT scans on a GE Discovery VCT scanner, 45-60 minutes post-injection of 10 mCi of [(18)F]FDG. Scans were acquired in both 2D and 3D modes. For each acquisition the raw PET data was reconstructed using five different reconstruction parameters. Lesions were segmented on a default image using the threshold of 40% of maximum SUV. Fifty different texture features were calculated inside the tumors. The range of variations of the features were calculated with respect to the average value.<h4>Results</h4>Fifty textural features were classified based on the range of variation in three categories: small, intermediate and large variability. Features with small variability (range ≤ 5%) were entropy-first order, energy, maximal correlation coefficient (second order feature) and low-gray level run emphasis (high-order feature). The features with intermediate variability (10% ≤ range ≤ 25%) were entropy-GLCM, sum entropy, high gray level run emphsis, gray level non-uniformity, small number emphasis, and entropy-NGL. Forty remaining features presented large variations (range > 30%).<h4>Conclusion</h4>Textural features such as entropy-first order, energy, maximal correlation coefficient, and low-gray level run emphasis exhibited small variations due to different acquisition modes and reconstruction parameters. Features with low level of variations are better candidates for reproducible tumor segmentation. Even though features such as contrast-NGTD, coarseness, homogeneity, and busyness have been previously used, our data indicated that these features presented large variations, therefore they could not be considered as a good candidates for tumor segmentation.", "affiliation": "Department of Medical Physics, University of Wisconsin, Madison, WI, USA. galavis@wisc.edu", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "research-article", "Evaluation Study", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "R01 CA136927", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "1R01CA136927", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Neoplasms", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "DG", "qualifierName": "diagnostic imaging"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Fluorodeoxyglucose F18"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Image Interpretation, Computer-Assisted", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "ST", "qualifierName": "standards"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Diagnostic Errors", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "SN", "qualifierName": "statistics & numerical data"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Observer Variation"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Tomography, Emission-Computed", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Positron-Emission Tomography", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "ST", "qualifierName": "standards"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Retrospective Studies"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Image Processing, Computer-Assisted", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "ST", "qualifierName": "standards"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "SN", "qualifierName": "statistics & numerical data"}]}}]}, "chemicalList": {"chemical": [{"name": "Fluorodeoxyglucose F18", "registryNumber": "0Z5B2CJX4D"}]}, "subsetList": {"subset": [{"code": "IM", "name": "Index Medicus"}]}, "fullTextUrlList": {"fullTextUrl": [{"availability": "Free", "availabilityCode": "F", "documentStyle": "doi", "site": "Unpaywall", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3109/0284186x.2010.498437"}, {"availability": "Subscription required", "av